I didn't talk about software copyright. I did talk about typeface
copyright. And typeface copyright is well established before courts.

If I purchase a (PostScript) font from Linotype Hell AG, I'm not
allowed to transform it to bitmap form to store it for further use.
The license agreement does not only cover the type itself, but also
its presentation.

It may be that this isn't the case with ATM, but at least that's
possible under German law.

